# 02 · Code Quality

## Quality Objective
Code should be readable, correct, and straightforward to modify safely.

## Baseline Standards
- Use the project's formatter and linter rules consistently.
- Treat warnings as work to triage, not permanent noise.
- Keep functions/classes focused on one clear responsibility.

## Readability Rules
- Name by intent, not implementation detail.
- Favor explicit control flow over clever compactness.
- Prefer immutable data where practical.
- Keep public APIs small and predictable.

## Error Handling
- Do not silently swallow errors.
- Convert low-level failures into actionable, user-safe errors at boundaries.
- Log operationally useful context without leaking sensitive data.

## Dependency Hygiene
- Add new dependencies only when built-in/project options are insufficient.
- Remove unused code, imports, and packages quickly.
- Keep dependency versions current and supported.

## Documentation
- Add comments for non-obvious "why", not obvious "what".
- Keep lightweight docs updated when introducing important behavior or constraints.
